Given, \(C=\dfrac{1}{2}~\text{F}\) The impedance of the capacitor in the Laplace domain is \[Z_C(s)
=
\frac{1}{Cs}
=
\frac{2}{s}\] \[\Rightarrow I_C(s)
=
\frac{V_C(s)}{Z_C(s)}
=
\frac{s(s+1)}
{2(s^3+s^2+s+1)}\] By the Initial Value Theorem, \[i_C(0^+)
=
\lim_{s\to\infty}sI_C(s)\] Substituting \(I_C(s)\), \[i_C(0^+)
=
\lim_{s\to\infty}
\frac{s^2(s+1)}
{2(s^3+s^2+s+1)}\] Dividing the numerator and denominator by \(s^3\), \[i_C(0^+)
=
\lim_{s\to\infty}
\frac{1+\dfrac{1}{s}}
{2\left(1+\dfrac{1}{s}+\dfrac{1}{s^2}+\dfrac{1}{s^3}\right)}=
\frac{1}{2}
~\text{A}\] Hence, the correct answer is \[\boxed{\textbf{C. }\frac{1}{2}~\text{A}}\]

For a transfer function \(H(s) = \frac{N(s)}{D(s)}\) of any stable, physical, linear time-invariant (LTI) two-port network, the location of poles and zeros in the complex frequency \(s\)-plane (\(\sigma + j\omega\)) must satisfy strict realizability and stability conditions. Let us evaluate the validity of each given statement: <ul><li><strong>Statement A is incorrect</strong>: For a standard network transfer function, zeros (roots of \(N(s) = 0\)) represent frequencies at which the network output is nullified. They can lie anywhere in the complex \(s\)-plane—including the right-half plane (RHP)—without violating causality or stability. Networks containing zeros in the right-half plane are termed non-minimum phase networks.</li><li><strong>Statement B is incorrect</strong>: The poles (roots of \(D(s) = 0\)) govern the natural response of the network. If any pole lies strictly in the right-half of the \(s\)-plane (\(\sigma &gt; 0\)), the natural response contains an exponentially growing term (\(e^{\sigma t}\)), causing the physical system to be unstable. Thus, poles cannot lie "anywhere" in the \(s\)-plane.</li><li><strong>Statement C is correct</strong>: If a system has a repeated (multiple-order) pole on the imaginary axis (\(j\omega\)-axis), the time-domain response will include a term of the form \(t \cdot \sin(\omega t)\) or \(t \cdot \cos(\omega t)\). Because of the multiplying factor \(t\), the amplitude grows infinitely over time, rendering the network unstable. Therefore, any poles lying on the imaginary axis must be simple (first-order) to yield a bounded, sustained sinusoidal response.</li><li><strong>Statement D is correct</strong>: A single, simple pole can reside at the origin (\(s = 0\)). This structurally represents a perfect integrator in system behavior (physically implemented by configurations like an ideal series capacitor or parallel inductor), which produces a stable bounded step response.</li></ul> Therefore, the correct options representing properties of a physical two-port network are C and D. \[\boxed{\textit{(c) the poles lying on the imaginary axis must be simple, (d) a pole may lie at origin}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>System Stability Summary</strong>: \[\begin{aligned}
    \text{Left-Half Plane (LHP) Poles} &amp;\implies \text{Absolutely Stable (Exponential Decay)} \\
    \text{Simple } j\omega\text{-axis / Origin Poles} &amp;\implies \text{Marginally Stable (Sustained Oscillation / Constant)} \\
    \text{Repeated } j\omega\text{-axis Poles} &amp;\implies \text{Unstable (Linearly Ramp-Amplified Output)} \\
    \text{Right-Half Plane (RHP) Poles} &amp;\implies \text{Unstable (Exponential Growth)}
    \end{aligned}\]</li><li>Zeros only alter the phase characteristics and minimum delay properties of the transfer network; their location does not jeopardize the bounded-input bounded-output (BIBO) stability framework.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>: Never mix the constraints of poles and zeros. Remember that poles handle stability (restricted boundary), whereas zeros handle phase minimums (unrestricted boundary except in specific driving-point functions).</li><li>: Do not assume all \(j\omega\)-axis poles mean instability. A single pole at the origin or a single pair of conjugate imaginary poles is perfectly permissible for a physical passive network. It only causes instability if those roots are repeated.</li></ul></div>

To evaluate the correct statements among the given digital IC logic families (<strong>ECL</strong>, <strong>TTL</strong>, and <strong>CMOS</strong>), we analyze the characteristic parameters of each option. Let us evaluate each statement individually based on standard semiconductor properties: <ul><li><strong>Statement A: ECL has the least propagation delay.</strong><br/><strong>Correct.</strong> Emitter Coupled Logic (ECL) is a non-saturating logic family where the transistors operate strictly within the active and cut-off regions. Because the transistors never enter saturation, there is no storage-time delay during switching transitions. This makes ECL the fastest logic family with the smallest propagation delay (\(\approx 1\,\text{ns}\)).</li><li><strong>Statement B: TTL has the largest fan-out.</strong><br/><strong>Incorrect.</strong> CMOS features an extremely high input impedance due to the insulated SiO\(_2\) gate structures of MOSFETs. Under static (DC) conditions, a CMOS gate draws negligible input current, allowing it to drive an exceptionally high number of similar gates. Hence, CMOS (not TTL) offers the highest static fan-out (\(\geq 50\)).</li><li><strong>Statement C: CMOS has the biggest noise margin.</strong><br/><strong>Correct.</strong> The noise margin of a logic family is proportional to its logic voltage swing. CMOS logic levels swing fully from \(0\,\text{V}\) to \(V_{DD}\). The typical noise margin for CMOS is roughly \(0.3V_{DD}\) to \(0.45V_{DD}\) (about \(1.5\,\text{V}\) to \(2.25\,\text{V}\) for a \(5\,\text{V}\) system), which is far superior to TTL (\(\approx 0.4\,\text{V}\)) and ECL (\(\approx 0.25\,\text{V}\)).</li><li><strong>Statement D: TTL has the lowest power consumption.</strong><br/><strong>Incorrect.</strong> CMOS technology exhibits the lowest static power consumption because one of the complementary transistors (nMOS or pMOS) is always turned off in steady-state, blocking any direct path from \(V_{DD}\) to ground.</li></ul> Thus, both statements <strong>A</strong> and <strong>C</strong> are technically accurate. \[\boxed{
    \begin{aligned}
    &amp;\text{(A)ECL has the least propagation delay} \\
    &amp;\text{(C)CMOS has the biggest noise margin}
    \end{aligned}
    }\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><div class="table-scroll"><table class="qtable"><tr><td><strong>Parameter</strong></td><td><strong>TTL</strong></td><td><strong>CMOS</strong></td><td><strong>ECL</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Propagation Delay</td><td>Medium (\(\approx 10\,\text{ns}\))</td><td>Medium (\(\approx 15\,\text{ns}\))</td><td><strong>Lowest</strong> (\(\approx 1\,\text{ns}\))</td></tr><tr><td>Power Dissipation</td><td>Medium (\(\approx 10\,\text{mW}\))</td><td><strong>Lowest</strong> (Static \(\approx \text{nW}\))</td><td>Highest (\(\approx 25\,\text{mW}\))</td></tr><tr><td>Noise Margin</td><td>Medium (\(\approx 0.4\,\text{V}\))</td><td><strong>Highest</strong> (\(\approx 1.5\,\text{V}\))</td><td>Lowest (\(\approx 0.25\,\text{V}\))</td></tr><tr><td>Fan-out</td><td>10</td><td><strong>Highest</strong> (\(&gt;50\))</td><td>25</td></tr></table></div></div>
